@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_Busy}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Accessibility}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Busy @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Busy @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</h1>

<p>
    The Busy Indicator component was built with accessibility and usability in mind, ensuring that all users are informed when an action is in progress and notified when it completes.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Inert content: </strong> While the indicator is active, all content beneath it (excluding the trigger button) is made inert, preventing keyboard and assistive technology 
    users from interacting with or navigating to elements that are temporarily unavailable.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Screen reader announcements: </strong> The component relies on the Live Region Service, part of the BlazorRamp.Core package, to communicate status to screen reader users. You are 
    required to provide a message for the completion of the action (optional start message); these are dispatched to hidden ARIA live regions and announced automatically without the user needing 
    to move their focus.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Reduced motion: </strong> Users who have the reduced motion preference enabled on their device will see a static hourglass instead of the animated spinning circle, respecting their 
    system-level accessibility settings.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong>These keyboard interactions are primarily aimed at keyboard-only users. Screen reader users will often navigate differently using their assistive technology's
    built-in commands, though the patterns also apply when a screen reader is operating in interaction mode.
</p>

<h2 id="device-testing">Accessibility device testing</h2>

<ul aria-labelledby="device-testing">
    <li>JAWS, Narrator and NVDA on Windows were each paired with every one of the following browsers: Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge and Mozilla Firefox.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on macOS was paired with Apple's Safari.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on iPhone was paired with mobile Safari.</li>
    <li>Talkback on Android was paired with mobile Google Chrome.</li>
    <li>Voice access on Windows 11</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> Testing was performed with the latest browser and assistive technologies versions that were available at the time of testing (see release history on the installation page for approximate testing dates)
</p>
